Chamfers and fillets on closed edges: the pieces of an analytic blend cut
over a gap by a face beyond (0055, 0071) on a closed spine, where the cut
is a conic and where the other contact runs along a closed edge
(mitcad#121): e.g. a ring standing on a block near its side, its foot
chamfered as wide as the ring is high, the chamfer's contact on the block
passing the side, which cuts the chamfer's cone; or a hole's rim near the
block's side, bevelled.

- ChFi3d_KPartCutCurve: the intersection lines of the blend and the face
  beyond are taken whether or not the intersection gives their p-curves
  (the arcs' p-curves are projected below anyway). A cone and a plane
  intersect in an analytic conic, a hyperbola, which comes without them
  and unbounded: such lines are not closed and have no ends to compare.
  Before, the cut was not found and the gap was left to the walking,
  whose chamfer rolling on an edge is not implemented.
- ChFi3d_FillKPartGaps: on a closed spine whose gaps were filled, a piece
  whose lines run across their period's end (from SplitKPart: the end's
  parameter before the start's) gets its end a period later. The data
  structure takes the parameters as they come, and the piece's face was
  built on the other part of its circles (inside out).
- runsAlongEdgePart (0054): a closed edge counts too. The contact of a
  closed spine along a closed edge comes in pieces, each along a part of
  the edge, so the face whose far edge it is (the ring's side) goes.
